 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Committed event revisions are pushed to browsers over server-managed WebSocket sessions, accepting connection-capacity and reconnect operations work in exchange for low-latency updates without recurring realtime-vendor fees or provider coupling. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected: although stateless and sufficient for the 15-second freshness goal, it adds several seconds of lag and repeated read load across simultaneous browsers. A hosted realtime service was rejected: although permitted by the infrastructure tenet and lighter on connection operations, it introduces recurring vendor costs and couples delivery and browser sync to a third-party subscription model. See [docs/decisions/change-delivery.md](decisions/change-delivery.md).
